74
00:07:00,120 --> 00:07:03,440
[Audience] How do you become a part of that process?

75
00:07:03,440 --> 00:07:05,960
[Audience] How do you become a participant in a process?

76
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:07,160
[Audience] Yes, a participant.

77
00:07:08,340 --> 00:07:11,500
[Kimbra White] You are randomly sampled of a database.

78
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:23,400
[Kimbra White] And then once people have expressed interest, so you might hope that you get three or four hundred people to express interest, and then they are selected again to match the demographics of the area in which you are.

79
00:07:23,400 --> 00:07:25,800
[Kimbra White] So it's actually random, stratified random.

80
00:07:25,800 --> 00:07:27,920
[Audience] So you can express your interest?

81
00:07:27,920 --> 00:07:32,460
[Kimbra White] Only if you, well, there are a couple of different methods of, it's called sortition.

82
00:07:32,460 --> 00:07:34,620
[Kimbra White] There are a couple of different methods for doing it.

83
00:07:34,660 --> 00:07:37,740
[Kimbra White] One is where a physical invitation goes out.

84
00:07:37,740 --> 00:07:42,120
[Kimbra White] Only those people who got the invitation can express interest, and that will be checked.

85
00:07:42,120 --> 00:07:43,440
[Kimbra White] You know, your address is checked.

86
00:07:43,860 --> 00:08:08,160
[Kimbra White] There's a second method that some people are using where you go out and you let absolutely everyone, you know, you just put ads out, you do social media, but you need to get hundreds and hundreds of people under that method to make sure you get the randomness in the next step when you're selecting, because you've got to make sure you get randomness rather than just the usual people who turn up all the time.

87
00:08:08,160 --> 00:08:10,380
[Audience] What is this database that you mentioned?

88
00:08:10,900 --> 00:08:13,860
[Kimbra White] The most typical one used is Australia Post.

89
00:08:13,860 --> 00:08:16,000
[Kimbra White] It's an Australia Post database.

90
00:08:17,160 --> 00:08:18,380
[Kimbra White] There are others used.

91
00:08:18,380 --> 00:08:30,040
[Kimbra White] So it depends on the sponsoring organisation, because like if it's a local council, they might have, they will may, I mean, a rate space is not good enough because you won't get the tenants.

92
00:08:30,040 --> 00:08:35,780
[Kimbra White] But they might have a lot of councils will have a full list of all addresses in their municipality.

93
00:08:37,220 --> 00:08:39,460
[Audience] So you address to the householder.

94
00:08:39,460 --> 00:08:40,880
[Kimbra White] Yes, address to the householder.

95
00:08:41,620 --> 00:08:42,220
[Kimbra White] Yeah.

96
00:08:42,220 --> 00:08:50,140
[Kimbra White] You could use a rate space and then you can address to a person, but then you're missing tenants and you'd have to find some way of picking up tenants.

97
00:08:50,140 --> 00:08:55,160
[Kimbra White] But there are people who are specialised in doing this, doing that sortition part of it.

98
00:08:55,640 --> 00:09:07,520
[Brian Khuu] So, as you hear, the aspects of citizen juries is definitely interesting because there's a lot of technical aspects that comes to making a citizen jury that can be trusted by the public.

99
00:09:07,540 --> 00:09:15,060
[Brian Khuu] I believe we also have another person, Willow, who runs a group called Coalition of Everyone.

100
