142
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:30,700
[Brian Khuu] The final topic we will be touching on is participatory budgeting.

143
00:12:30,700 --> 00:12:55,580
[Brian Khuu] Admittedly, I'm not a very well-versed in the concept of participatory budgeting, but from my understanding, it's about having a sort of a process of deliberation or maybe electronic voting, which allows people to define the nation's budget to where which industry needs to be funded, or the health care system, or the hospitals.

144
00:12:55,580 --> 00:12:57,380
[Kimbra White] Do you want me to say more?

145
00:12:57,380 --> 00:12:59,760
[Brian Khuu] I would love to have your input.

146
00:12:59,780 --> 00:13:01,060
[Alexar Pendashteh?] Is Kimbra speaking, yeah?

147
00:13:01,060 --> 00:13:01,520
[Kimbra White] Yeah.

148
00:13:01,520 --> 00:13:04,800
[Kimbra White] So participatory budgeting is...

149
00:13:04,800 --> 00:13:07,740
[Kimbra White] It would be lovely if it was seen on a national scale.

150
00:13:07,740 --> 00:13:12,020
[Kimbra White] I have never seen anyone do it at that scale or for a whole budget.

151
00:13:12,120 --> 00:13:13,380
[Kimbra White] It's really...

152
00:13:13,380 --> 00:13:15,420
[Kimbra White] The take up of it has been...

153
00:13:15,420 --> 00:13:22,100
[Kimbra White] It is about spending public money, and it is about involving community citizens, people.

154
00:13:22,100 --> 00:13:26,740
[Kimbra White] Sometimes we don't use the word citizens, because there's an assumption that they have got the paperwork.

155
00:13:26,740 --> 00:13:32,380
[Kimbra White] Also, using ordinary people to make a decision about where a budget is spent.

156
00:13:32,380 --> 00:13:34,240
[Kimbra White] It came out of Brazil.

157
00:13:34,240 --> 00:13:38,240
[Kimbra White] It's been taken up quite widely in Europe, particularly in the UK.

158
00:13:38,240 --> 00:13:40,720
[Kimbra White] There's been very little proper take up of it.

159
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:44,020
[Kimbra White] There's not a lot of take up of it in Australia.

160
00:13:44,020 --> 00:14:03,580
[Kimbra White] In the UK, there's a very strong, and probably in South America, that people need to turn up to a place that you come along and as a group of people together, you look at all the different ways people want to spend a budget, and then you talk about that and vote on that in a face-to-face process.

161
00:14:03,580 --> 00:14:12,680
[Kimbra White] There is a tech version of it where it's done online, and that has had a little bit of take-up in Australia.

162
00:14:12,680 --> 00:14:20,780
[Kimbra White] So the biggest project I know is the Victorian government's pick-my-project process that happened last year, where community groups could...

163
00:14:20,780 --> 00:14:22,460
[Kimbra White] There was a bucket of money.

164
00:14:22,460 --> 00:14:30,940
[Kimbra White] Community groups could seek to get some of that money, and everyone who was involved voted on where the money should go.

165
00:14:30,940 --> 00:14:33,100
[Kimbra White] But it was an online process.

166
00:14:33,100 --> 00:14:40,700
[Kimbra White] Melville Council in Western Australia has been doing a project, they call the Robin Hood Project, probably from the last five to six years.

167
00:14:40,700 --> 00:14:41,780
[Brian Khuu?] That's pretty fascinating.

168
00:14:41,780 --> 00:14:49,940
[Brian Khuu?] So it almost sounds like that participatory budgeting doesn't necessarily have to be for the nation, but it could even be for, say, a charity.

169
00:14:49,940 --> 00:14:52,560
[Kimbra White] Yes, it's far more typical for...

170
00:14:53,120 --> 00:14:55,960
[Kimbra White] Most local councils have a community grants program.

171
00:14:55,960 --> 00:14:59,560
[Kimbra White] It is much more focused on topics like that.

172
00:14:59,560 --> 00:15:03,620
[Kimbra White] I haven't seen anyone seriously talk about budgets.

173
00:15:03,860 --> 00:15:13,400
[Kimbra White] Some people would say that the City of Melbourne's 10-year financial plan deliberation was a participatory budgeting process because it was a deliberation around a budget.

174
00:15:13,400 --> 00:15:17,040
[Kimbra White] I would say it was a deliberation, not a PV process.

175
00:15:17,040 --> 00:15:18,660
[Kimbra White] But, you know, it's definitions.

176
00:15:18,660 --> 00:15:20,400
[Kimbra White] You know, what's a citizens assembly?

177
00:15:20,400 --> 00:15:22,660
[Kimbra White] What's a people's assembly?

178
00:15:22,660 --> 00:15:39,000
[Kimbra White] You know, there's a whole lot of definitional stuff around this because people who really believe in the, you know, the people that, you know, are out promoting deliberation would say that, you know, it's got to be five to six full days of deliberation, you know, and if it's not that, you don't follow the deliberation.

